(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_cellcycleandcelldivision=[{id:"neet-2026-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. G\u2081 phase \u2014 1. Actual cell division occurs
B. S phase \u2014 II. Cell is metabolically active and continuously grows but does not replicate its DNA
C. G\u2082 phase \u2014 III. Synthesis of DNA occurs and the amount of DNA per cell doubles
D. M phase \u2014 IV. Proteins are synthesized while cell growth continues
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],c:0,s:`In G\u2081 phase the cell is metabolically active and grows continuously but does not replicate its DNA.
S phase is when DNA synthesis (replication) takes place and the amount of DNA per cell doubles.
In G\u2082 phase proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis while cell growth continues.
In M phase the actual cell division takes place.
So A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q106",pn:106,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:"The correct sequence of adult cell cycle phases is ________.",o:["G1-M-G2-S","G1-S-G2-M","S-M-G2-G1","G1-G2-S-M"],c:1,s:`Gap 1 (G1) is the first phase of interphase.
After G1, the synthesis (S) phase starts and DNA replicates.
Then the Gap 2 (G2) phase occurs.
Finally the cell cycle ends with M phase (mitosis).
Sequence: G1-S-G2-M.`,sfig:{w:385,h:345,alt:"Cell cycle circle: G\u2081 (largest sector), then S, then G\u2082, then a narrow M Phase sector divided into Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase and Cytokinesis; a thick arrow runs clockwise round the outer ring; cells leave the cycle at the top into G\u2080.",d:[["c",240,205,125,""],["c",240,205,108,""],["p","M 240 205 L 170.6 122.3 A 108 108 0 0 1 346.4 186.2 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 346.4 186.2 A 108 108 0 0 1 285.6 302.9 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 285.6 302.9 A 108 108 0 0 1 138.5 241.9 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 138.5 241.9 A 108 108 0 0 1 133.1 220.0 Z","f"],["l",104,216,161.4,224.6,"d"],["t",100,219,"Prophase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 133.1 220.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 132.3 197.5 Z","f"],["l",104,202,159,207.8,"d"],["t",100,205,"Metaphase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 132.3 197.5 A 108 108 0 0 1 136.2 175.2 Z","f"],["l",104,188,160.2,190.9,"d"],["t",100,191,"Anaphase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 136.2 175.2 A 108 108 0 0 1 144.6 154.3 Z","f"],["l",104,174,164.9,174.7,"d"],["t",100,177,"Telophase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 144.6 154.3 A 108 108 0 0 1 170.6 122.3 Z","f"],["l",104,160,177.1,154,"d"],["t",100,163,"Cytokinesis","es"],["p","M 130.5 244.8 A 116.5 116.5 0 0 1 165.1 115.8","B"],["p","M 163.7 96.1 A 133 133 0 0 1 263.1 74.0","Ba"],["t",260.3,149.2,"G\u2081","m"],["t",297.7,238.4,"S","m"],["t",214.9,262.8,"G\u2082","m"],["t",8,115,"M Phase",""],["c",137.6,91.3,9,"f"],["c",138.6,92.3,3.5,"F"],["c",170.5,68.7,9,"f"],["c",171.5,69.7,3.5,"F"],["c",261.3,53.5,9,"f"],["c",262.3,54.5,3.5,"F"],["p","M 299.3 45.5 A 10 10 0 1 0 285.3 43.5","a"],["t",291.3,55.5,"G\u2080","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q109",pn:109,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Chromosomes are fully condensed at the end of prophase I.
Statement II : Meiosis I resembles mitosis.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct, but Statement II is false","Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],c:1,s:`The last stage of meiotic prophase I is diakinesis.
At the end of prophase I the chromosomes are fully condensed and the meiotic spindle is assembled, ready to separate the homologous chromosomes.
So Statement I is correct.
It is meiosis II that resembles mitosis, as it is an equational division.
So Statement II is false.`},{id:"neet-2025-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Mitosis spindle",q:"What is the main function of the spindle fibers during mitosis?",o:["To separate the chromosomes","To synthesize new DNA","To repair damaged DNA","To regulate cell growth"],c:0,s:`During mitosis the spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores of the chromosomes.
They help in the separation of the chromosomes.`},{id:"neet-2024-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Metaphase",q:"Spindle fibers attach to kinetochores of chromosomes during",o:["Anaphase","Telophase","Prophase","Metaphase"],c:3,s:"Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores of the chromosomes at metaphase."},{id:"neet-2024-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis prophase I",q:`Given below are two Statements:
Statement I : Chromomes become gradually visible under light microscope during leptotene stage.
Statement II: The begining of diplotene stage is recognized by dissolution of synaptonemal complex.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],c:2,s:`In leptotene the chromosomes slowly become visible under the light microscope.
The start of diplotene is marked by dissolution of the synaptonemal complex.
Also, the recombined homologous chromosomes of the bivalents tend to separate, except at the sites of crossing over.
So both statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2024-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Cell cycle phases",q:`Following are the stages of cell division :
A. Gap 2 phase
B. Cytokinesis
C. Synthesis phase
D. Karyokinesis
E. Gap 1 phase
Choose the correct sequence of stages from the options given below :`,o:["B-D-E-A-C","E-C-A-D-B","C-E-D-A-B","E-B-D-A-C"],c:1,s:`The order is Gap 1 phase (E), then Synthesis phase (C), then Gap 2 phase (A).
Then Karyokinesis (D), then Cytokinesis (B).
So E \u2192 C \u2192 A \u2192 D \u2192 B.`},{id:"neet-2024-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Prophase I sub-stages",q:`Match List I with List II: (Sub Phases of Prophase I \u2014 Specific characters)
A. Diakinesis \u2014 I. Synaptonemal complex formation
B. Pachytene \u2014 II. Completion of terminalisation of chiasmata
C. Zygotene \u2014 III. Chromosomes look like thin threads
D. Leptotene \u2014 IV. Appearance of recombination nodules`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],c:0,s:`Diakinesis: terminalisation of chiasmata is completed.
Pachytene: recombination nodules appear.
Zygotene: synaptonemal complex forms.
Leptotene: chromosomes look like thin threads.
So A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2023-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis stages",q:"Which of the following stages of stages of meiosis involves division of centromere ?",o:["Telophase","Metaphase-I","Metaphase-II","Anaphase-II"],c:3,s:`The centromere splits in anaphase of mitosis and in anaphase II of meiosis.
In metaphase I and II, chromosomes only line up at the equator.
In telophase, chromosomes have already reached the poles.`},{id:"neet-2023-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis prophase I substages",q:"The process of appearance of recombination nodules occurs at which sub stage of prophase I in meiosis ?",o:["Diakinesis","Zygotene","Pachytene","Diplotene"],c:2,s:`Recombination takes place in the pachytene stage of prophase I.
This stage shows recombination nodules.`},{id:"neet-2023-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:"Among eukaryotes, replication of DNA takes place in :",o:["G2 phase","M phase","S phase","G1 phase"],c:2,s:`DNA replication in eukaryotes happens in the S phase.
Most cell organelles duplicate in the G1 phase.`},{id:"neet-2023-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:`Match List I with List II :
List I: A. M Phase; B. G2 Phase; C. Quiescent stage; D. G1 Phase
List II: I. Proteins are synthesized; II. Inactive phase; III. Interval between mitosis and initiation of DNA replication; IV. Equational division
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],c:3,s:`M phase is mitosis, the equational division: A-IV.
In G2, DNA synthesis stops but the cell makes RNA and proteins for the next phase: B-I.
Quiescent stage is the inactive phase entered by non-dividing cells: C-II.
G1 is the gap between mitosis and start of DNA replication: D-III.`},{id:"neet-2023-q198",pn:198,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Stages of meiosis and mitosis",q:`Select the correct statements.
A. Tetrad formation is seen during Leptotene.
B. During Anaphase, the centromeres split and chromatids separate.
C. Terminalization takes place during Pachytene.
D. Nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER are reformed during Telophase.
E. Crossing over takes place between sister chromatids of homologous chromosome.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and E only","A and C only","B and D only","A, C and E only"],c:2,s:`B and D are correct.
A is wrong: tetrads form in zygotene.
C is wrong: terminalisation of chiasmata happens in diakinesis.
E is wrong: crossing over is between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.`},{id:"neet-2023-q200",pn:200,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : During G0 phase of cell cycle, the cell is metabolically inactive.
Statement II : The centrosome undergoes duplication during S phase of interphase.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."],c:0,s:`Cells in G0 are metabolically active but do not divide unless needed. Statement I is incorrect.
In animal cells the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm during S phase. Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q102",pn:102,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis prophase I",q:"Which stage of meiosis can last for months or years in the oocytes of some vertebrates?",o:["Diakinesis","Leptotene","Pachytene","Diplotene"],c:3,s:`In the oocytes of some vertebrates the diplotene stage can last months or years.
This suspended diplotene is called the dictyotene state.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q107",pn:107,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Crossing over enzyme",q:"In meiosis, crossing over and exchange of material between homologous chromosomes catalyzed by the enzyme:",o:["Polymerase","Phosphorylase","Recombinase","Transferase"],c:2,s:"Recombinase is the enzyme needed for crossing over and exchange of genetic material."},{id:"neet-2022-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Mitosis",q:"Which one of the following never occurs during mitotic cell division?",o:["Movement of centrioles towards opposite poles","Pairing of homologous chromosomes","Coiling and condensation of the chromatids","Spindle fibres attach to kinetochores of chromosomes"],c:1,s:`Pairing of homologous chromosomes happens in prophase I of meiosis.
The other three events occur in both mitosis and meiosis.`},{id:"neet-2022-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Recombination nodules in pachytene",q:"The appearance of recombination nodules on homologous chromosomes during meiosis characterizes :",o:["Bivalent","Sites at which crossing over occurs","Terminalization","Synaptonemal complex"],c:1,s:`Recombination nodules appear in the pachytene stage of meiosis.
They mark the sites where crossing over takes place.
Crossing over happens between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.`},{id:"neet-2022-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Types of chromosomes",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) Metacentric chromosome (b) Acrocentric chromosome (c) Submetacentric (d) Telocentric chromosome
List-II: (i) Centromere situated close to the end forming one extremely short and one very long arms (ii) Centromere at the terminal end (iii) Centromere in the middle forming two equal arms of chromosomes (iv) Centromere slightly away from the middle forming one shorter arm and one longer arm
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],c:3,s:`Metacentric: centromere in the middle, two equal arms: (a)-(iii).
Acrocentric: centromere near the end, one very short and one very long arm: (b)-(i).
Submetacentric: centromere slightly off the middle, one shorter and one longer arm: (c)-(iv).
Telocentric: centromere at the terminal end: (d)-(ii).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q145",pn:145,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Prophase I stages",q:`Identify the correct sequence of events during Prophase I of meiosis :
(a) Synapsis of homologous chromosomes
(b) Chromosomes become gradually visible under microscope
(c) Crossing over between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes
(d) Terminalisation of chiasmata
(e) Dissolution of synaptonemal complex
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a), (c), (d), (e), (b)","(a), (b), (c), (d), (e)","(b), (c), (d), (e), (a)","(b), (a), (c), (e), (d)"],c:3,s:`First the chromatin condenses and chromosomes become visible.
Homologous chromosomes then pair up (synapsis).
Crossing over exchanges segments between non-sister chromatids.
The synaptonemal complex then dissolves and the chromosomes start to move apart.
The crossing-over points show as X-shaped chiasmata, which finally terminalise.
Note: Printed key (4) gives the order b, a, c, e, d; the printed solution lists terminalisation after the dissolution of the complex, which matches it.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q156",pn:156,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis prophase I stages",q:"Bivalent or Tetrad formation is a characteristic feature observed during:",o:["Chiasmata in zygotene stage","Synaptonemal complex in zygotene stage","Chiasmata in Diplotene stage","Synaptonemal complex in Pachytene stage"],c:3,s:`Bivalents (tetrads) are seen in the pachytene stage.
At this stage the homologous chromosomes undergo crossing over.`},{id:"neet-2022-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Mitosis stages",q:"Select the incorrect statement with reference to mitosis:",o:["Spindle fibres attach to centromere of chromosomes","Chromosomes decondense at telophase","Splitting of centromere occurs at anaphase","All the chromosomes lie at the equator at metaphase"],c:0,s:`Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores, not directly to the centromere.
Kinetochores are disc-shaped structures on the sides of the primary constriction (centromere).
So option (1) is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis",q:"Regarding Meiosis, which of the statements in incorrect?",o:["DNA replication occurs in S phase of Meiosis \u2013 II","Pairing of homologous chromosomes and recombination occurs in Meiosis \u2013 I","Four haploid cells are formed at the end of Meiosis \u2013 II","There are two stages in Meiosis, Meiosis \u2013 I and II"],c:0,s:`Meiosis has two sequential divisions, meiosis I and II, but only one round of DNA replication.
The short stage between them is interkinesis, with no DNA replication.
So there is no S phase in meiosis II.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q186",pn:186,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Metaphase",q:"With respect to metaphase, which of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["Chromosomes lie at the equator of the cell","Complete disintegration of nuclear envelope takes place","Chromosomes are highly condensed","Metaphase chromosomes are made up of four sister chromatids held together by centromere"],c:3,s:`Each metaphase chromosome has only two sister chromatids joined at the centromere.
So 'four' is wrong.`}]})();
